theory.intro:
A sinusoidal model $y=k\cos(at)+c$ is built from three features of the situation. The **amplitude** $A=|k|$ is half the gap between the maximum and the minimum, the **mean position** $c$ sits halfway between them, and $a$ comes from the period. The coefficient $k$ is signed: a maximum at $t=0$ requires $k>0$, while a minimum at $t=0$ requires $k<0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.3,0) -- (8.4,0) node[right] {$t$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,2.5) -- (8,2.5);
\node[left,font=\small] at (0,2.5) {$c$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0:8,samples=120,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.5+1.2*cos(60*\x)});
\draw[<->] (3,2.5) -- (3,1.3) node[midway,right,font=\small] {$A=|k|$};
\draw[<->] (0,4.3) -- (6,4.3) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The amplitude and mean position are
$\begin{aligned}A&=\frac{1400-800}{2}\\&=300\end{aligned}$
$\begin{aligned}c&=\frac{1400+800}{2}\\&=1100\end{aligned}$
The period is $12$ months, so
$\begin{aligned}a&=\frac{2\pi}{12}\\&=\frac{\pi}{6}\end{aligned}$
The population starts at its maximum, so $k=300$.
$P(t)=300\cos\left(\frac{\pi t}{6}\right)+1100$.
The sketch shows one cycle, with the mean position marked. The vertical-axis break indicates a truncated population scale.
